1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,920
The school Saturday covers sepsis recognition and anaphylaxis, while
Wednesday emphasizes early maternal sepsis and useful differential
checks. This workshop joins those tasks through clinical decisions.

2
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:24,216
Both conditions can impair vascular tone, filling and tissue
perfusion. Similar-looking shock does not mean that the first cause-
specific treatment is the same.

3
00:00:24,216 --> 00:00:35,160
We will distinguish screening from diagnosis, recognize danger before
a late pressure collapse, and practise prompt IM epinephrine when
anaphylaxis is indicated.

4
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:47,880
The original cases are rehearsal, not school assessment answers. Use
the paired main-text and assigned examination reading without blending
old and current instructions into one invented protocol.

5
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:59,016
An infection can be localized, but a dysregulated host response can
injure organs beyond the source. You do not need proof that organisms
are circulating everywhere.

6
00:00:59,016 --> 00:01:11,664
Sepsis concerns life-threatening organ dysfunction related to that
response. New confusion, worsening breathing or poor perfusion may be
more important than the temperature alone.

7
00:01:11,664 --> 00:01:23,712
Fever is not required, and the cause is not restricted to gram-
negative bacteria. A culture result is usually unavailable in the
field and is not a prerequisite for clinical concern.

8
00:01:23,712 --> 00:01:35,568
A suspected source plus otherwise unexplained dysfunction needs timely
evaluation. Continue to consider a noninfectious cause rather than
using the sepsis label to close reasoning.

9
00:01:35,568 --> 00:01:46,680
Vasodilation increases vascular capacity and can reduce effective
pressure and return. Capillary leakage moves plasma fluid out of the
circulation into tissues.

10
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:58,080
Microvascular distribution and oxygen use may be impaired even when a
cuff pressure is not yet critically low. Myocardial dysfunction can
further complicate the circulation.

11
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:09,456
Edema does not prove the vascular space is well filled. At the same
time, a poorly perfused edematous patient is not automatically a
candidate for unlimited fluid.

12
00:02:09,456 --> 00:02:20,424
This is a mechanism for reassessment, not a rigid warm-then-cold shock
timetable. Use the actual function, flow clues and response to guide
the next decision.

13
00:02:20,424 --> 00:02:30,552
Ask what the patient could do yesterday and what changed today. New
confusion or inability to manage usual tasks can be a meaningful early
change.

14
00:02:30,552 --> 00:02:44,472
Assess breathing rate, depth, effort, air movement and oxygenation,
not only saturation. Organ dysfunction can present as a respiratory
problem before hypotension is obvious.

15
00:02:44,472 --> 00:02:56,160
Compare peripheral perfusion, pulses and pressure trends. A warm
appearance does not establish safety, and cool or mottled skin is not
a required late-stage sequence.

16
00:02:56,160 --> 00:03:09,336
Reduced urine output can add concern, but not every AKI is oliguric
and a field estimate is imperfect. Preserve the history and
uncertainty rather than claiming a laboratory diagnosis.

17
00:03:09,336 --> 00:03:19,080
The school specifically asks you to recognize early SIRS findings.
Learn the criteria accurately and say which observations are actually
available.

18
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:30,816
SIRS can occur with infection, but also with trauma, burns or
pancreatitis. Two inflammatory findings do not by themselves prove the
infectious cause or severity.

19
00:03:30,816 --> 00:03:40,872
The Nancy SIRS-plus-infection formula is historical diagnostic
wording. It should not replace current recognition of infection-
associated organ dysfunction.

20
00:03:40,872 --> 00:03:51,432
Use the exercise to notice change earlier, not to wait until two
perfect boxes or a laboratory result are available before addressing a
deteriorating patient.

21
00:03:51,432 --> 00:03:59,544
The directly read Nancy criteria include temperature above thirty-
eight or below thirty-six, and heart rate above ninety per minute.

22
00:03:59,544 --> 00:04:11,184
The respiratory criterion is a rate above twenty or arterial carbon
dioxide below thirty-two. An end-tidal reading is not automatically
the same as that arterial criterion.

23
00:04:11,184 --> 00:04:23,064
The book white-cell line is above twelve or below four times ten to
the ninth per liter. Other standard versions may add immature cells;
never invent an unavailable field count.

24
00:04:23,064 --> 00:04:34,368
State the observed components and whether the historical two-or-more
screen is met. Missing data remain unknown, and the result still needs
cause and organ-function assessment.

25
00:04:34,368 --> 00:04:46,008
A low score cannot safely overrule new dysfunction or a worrying
trend. A high score may be caused by another disease and is not an
automatic sepsis diagnosis.

26
00:04:46,008 --> 00:04:58,440
The selected twenty-twenty-six SSC guidance supports standardized
prehospital screening and cautions against sole qSOFA use for hospital
screening. It does not turn qSOFA into a rule-out.

27
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:09,648
Likewise, one normal lactate or biomarker does not prove absence of
disease. A raised value can reflect more than one mechanism and
requires interpretation.

28
00:05:09,648 --> 00:05:18,336
If the patient worries you, repeat the assessment, seek support and
communicate the concern even when the formal score seems reassuring.

29
00:05:18,336 --> 00:05:31,320
In the intended adult population, use the approved chart and measure
each physiological component. Include oxygen use and the correct
saturation scale rather than calculating from an incomplete row.

30
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:41,952
New confusion is a major finding even if the patient opens eyes and
talks. Do not call the consciousness score normal merely because
conversation is possible.

31
00:05:41,952 --> 00:05:55,152
A single component scoring three can matter even when the total is not
extreme. The scoring and response principles from Week One remain,
with local service actions distinguished from hospital staffing
intervals.

32
00:05:55,152 --> 00:06:06,072
Record the component values with the total so the receiving team can
interpret the change. A score without measurements, time or treatment
context is weak communication.

33
00:06:06,072 --> 00:06:19,968
The original RCP recommendations exclude children under sixteen and
pregnant patients, while a FAQ discusses a twenty-week boundary. That
wording difference is not proof of universal obstetric validation.

34
00:06:19,968 --> 00:06:30,456
K zero two uses broader all-patient wording. Do not resolve the
difference by inventing a low-score clearance for a pregnant or
recently postpartum patient.

35
00:06:30,456 --> 00:06:42,792
The source tool reserves saturation scale two for the appropriate
clinician-selected hypercapnic respiratory-failure context. It is not
automatically selected from every COPD label.

36
00:06:42,792 --> 00:06:54,576
Use the actual local maternity assessment and consultation process.
Physiology, source symptoms, organ function and clinical concern
remain essential regardless of the score.

37
00:06:54,576 --> 00:07:06,216
Check glucose in suspected sepsis and altered function. Treat
confirmed hypoglycemia through the actual airway-safe pathway, then
repeat glucose and the clinical assessment.

38
00:07:06,216 --> 00:07:15,720
A high glucose without known diabetes may be a stress clue, but it
does not identify infection or eliminate DKA and other alternatives.

39
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:27,696
Lactate can reflect several processes, including altered production
and clearance. It is not a direct meter of one patient universal
oxygen debt or a sepsis-only result.

40
00:07:27,696 --> 00:07:38,928
Use only actually available authorized tests. A normal result does not
cancel worsening function, and collecting a test must not delay
necessary support or conveyance.

41
00:07:38,928 --> 00:07:51,240
Ask focused questions about respiratory, urinary, abdominal and skin
symptoms, recent procedures, devices and treatment. A suspected source
should fit the time course.

42
00:07:51,240 --> 00:08:02,712
Malodorous urine alone does not establish a urinary infection.
Similarly, a visible wound is not proof that it explains every
neurological or circulatory finding.

43
00:08:02,712 --> 00:08:15,096
Examine for mottling, non-blanching rash or rapidly progressive
painful tissue changes where indicated, with appropriate consent and
PPE. Do not require a rash for recognition.

44
00:08:15,096 --> 00:08:26,208
Ask about antipyretics and antimicrobials, prior results and access
barriers without blame. Current treatment does not guarantee that a
new serious infection is controlled.

45
00:08:26,208 --> 00:08:37,056
A fifty-one-year-old with a worsening hand wound after a repair
workshop is now confused and cannot follow familiar tasks. The
temperature is thirty-six point three.

46
00:08:37,056 --> 00:08:48,672
Pulse is one hundred eighteen, respiratory rate twenty-six, pressure
one hundred four over sixty-six, saturation ninety-six on room air and
glucose six point eight.

47
00:08:48,672 --> 00:08:59,040
The absence of fever does not explain away the new dysfunction. A
possible infectious source, rapid breathing and altered function
require urgent assessment.

48
00:08:59,040 --> 00:09:11,136
Keep other causes open, including neurological, toxic or metabolic
illness. This is suspected infection-related dysfunction, not a field-
confirmed culture diagnosis.

49
00:09:11,136 --> 00:09:22,968
In the historical SIRS exercise, the observed pulse and breathing rate
are positive; the temperature is not and the white-cell count is
unknown. Do not invent the missing count.

50
00:09:22,968 --> 00:09:37,416
For this nonpregnant adult using scale one, NEWS two is nine: three
for breathing, one for pressure, two for pulse and three for new
confusion. The remaining measured components and room air add zero.

51
00:09:37,416 --> 00:09:50,016
The score supports deterioration concern, but the clinical change was
already enough to act. Do not wait until systolic pressure falls below
ninety before organizing support and conveyance.

52
00:09:50,016 --> 00:10:00,936
Report the possible source, new functional change, actual measurements
and response. Reassess the patient rather than simply repeating the
number nine to every team member.

53
00:10:00,936 --> 00:10:12,624
Support the actual airway and ventilation problems immediately. Oxygen
should meet the applicable patient/pathway target, not a generic all-
patient high-flow ritual.

54
00:10:12,624 --> 00:10:23,904
Do not walk a poorly perfused patient. Use a position suited to
circulation and breathing, protect against heat loss where appropriate
and avoid unnecessary exertion.

55
00:10:23,904 --> 00:10:35,832
If pressure support is considered, its own eligibility and
contraindications still apply. The word sepsis does not make every
hypoxic or hypotensive patient suitable for CPAP.

56
00:10:35,832 --> 00:10:46,896
Assign someone to repeat observations and call out change while egress
and receiving arrangements proceed. Do not finish a perfect checklist
while the main threat worsens.

57
00:10:46,896 --> 00:10:58,248
Obtain indicated access and use the actual service fluid plan. The
patient may be depleted even with chronic edema, but that does not
remove the risk of overload.

58
00:10:58,248 --> 00:11:09,720
Assess the response frequently: mentation, pulse, pressure, skin and
breathing. New respiratory worsening or edema needs immediate review
of the plan.

59
00:11:09,720 --> 00:11:21,960
International adult or maternal volume examples are not automatic
ordinary PCP orders. Do not force one fixed amount on every renal,
cardiac or pregnant patient.

60
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:34,392
Persistent shock needs early qualified assistance, not endless
repeated fluid without a response assessment. Continue timely
conveyance and notification rather than waiting for a bag to finish.

61
00:11:34,392 --> 00:11:46,632
Antimicrobial treatment and source control address the infection
itself. Early communication can help the receiving team prepare those
resources without waiting for a formal field confirmation.

62
00:11:46,632 --> 00:11:58,560
The selected twenty-twenty-six SSC statements distinguish levels of
infection likelihood and shock, rather than giving every possible
infection the same automatic antibiotic clock.

63
00:11:58,560 --> 00:12:13,584
Its prehospital antibiotic suggestion requires a structured program,
appropriate shock context and prolonged time to medical evaluation. It
does not authorize an ordinary BC PCP to select independent
antibiotics.

64
00:12:13,584 --> 00:12:27,384
State suspected infection, the organ/perfusion problem, relevant
history, actual treatment and response, ETA and any capability
request. That useful alert is within the current task now.

65
00:12:27,384 --> 00:12:39,816
A thirty-nine-year-old ten days after delivery had been recovering,
but now has new confusion, increasing pelvic pain and abnormal
discharge. Temperature is thirty-eight point four.

66
00:12:39,816 --> 00:12:51,648
Pulse is one hundred twenty-six, breathing twenty-six and pressure one
hundred ten over seventy. Do not dismiss the pattern as normal
postpartum physiology or wait for shock.

67
00:12:51,648 --> 00:13:05,760
Support current threats, check glucose and evaluate bleeding,
neurological/hypertensive findings and possible source. Use the actual
maternity pathway rather than a generic score as clearance.

68
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:17,112
A working maternal-sepsis concern needs early notification, while
hemorrhage and other causes remain active alternatives. Preserve the
delivery date and symptom-change timeline.

69
00:13:17,112 --> 00:13:28,248
Rank an alternative that could harm this patient if missed, not a list
of unrelated diagnoses. Use the actual pregnancy, exposure and symptom
chronology.

70
00:13:28,248 --> 00:13:39,960
A glucose measurement, bleeding check, pressure/neurological
reassessment or focused exposure question can be a useful
discriminator, provided it does not postpone rescue.

71
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:49,392
State why the alternative matters and what finding would alter the
plan. A spoken What Else with no connection to an action is only a
slogan.

72
00:13:49,392 --> 00:14:01,728
Revisit the explanation after every meaningful change. Infection and
another problem may coexist, just as anaphylaxis may produce a GI or
neurological-looking complaint.

73
00:14:01,728 --> 00:14:11,352
Anaphylaxis is an acute potentially life-threatening hypersensitivity
presentation. Mediator release can affect several systems at the same
time.

74
00:14:11,352 --> 00:14:22,200
Upper-airway swelling can change voice or produce stridor, while
lower-airway bronchospasm and mucus impair airflow. These are related
but distinct threats.

75
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:34,128
Vasodilation and plasma leakage reduce effective circulating volume
and return, with possible cardiac effects. Severe compromise does not
require external blood loss.

76
00:14:34,128 --> 00:14:45,000
This explains why indicated IM epinephrine and ABC support run in
parallel. One bronchodilator or antihistamine cannot replace the full
rescue plan.

77
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:55,344
In common IgE-mediated reactions, previous sensitization prepares mast
cells and basophils to release mediators when the allergen is
encountered again.

78
00:14:55,344 --> 00:15:05,136
The patient may not know that sensitization occurred. A first
recognized reaction or a report of no known allergy does not exclude
the syndrome.

79
00:15:05,136 --> 00:15:15,408
Non-IgE mechanisms can produce anaphylaxis as well. Do not require a
specific antibody explanation before treating an indicated clinical
emergency.

80
00:15:15,408 --> 00:15:28,320
Repeated exposures do not follow a guaranteed worsening staircase.
Base urgency on today actual airway, breathing, circulation and
progression rather than a prediction from last time.

81
00:15:28,320 --> 00:15:39,672
Ask about voice change, a throat lump or difficulty speaking and
inspect relevant swelling. Stridor suggests an upper-airway threat
different from a lower-airway wheeze.

82
00:15:39,672 --> 00:15:50,880
Assess respiratory rate, depth, work and air entry. A noisy chest may
become quiet because less air moves, not because the condition has
improved.

83
00:15:50,880 --> 00:16:03,576
Fatigue, ineffective effort or declining consciousness requires
immediate support and skilled assistance. A normal saturation at one
instant must not cancel these observations.

84
00:16:03,576 --> 00:16:14,496
Do not wait for a completely obstructed airway to request resources or
give indicated IM treatment. Recognizing the mechanism should shorten,
not lengthen, the delay.

85
00:16:14,496 --> 00:16:26,664
Rapid vasodilation and leakage can reduce return and output.
Faintness, collapse or altered function after a plausible exposure may
be a circulatory emergency.

86
00:16:26,664 --> 00:16:39,168
Skin findings may be subtle, absent, earlier than arrival or partly
masked. Do not wait for a photogenic rash or a pressure below ninety
before recognizing dangerous compromise.

87
00:16:39,168 --> 00:16:50,280
The patient may also have cramping, vomiting or diarrhea. A GI chief
complaint does not guarantee a GI-only cause if airway or perfusion
features are developing.

88
00:16:50,280 --> 00:17:00,720
Treat the indicated syndrome while assessing other possibilities. The
aim is useful early action with reassessment, not a definitive
allergen diagnosis at the scene.

89
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:11,712
Contemporary criteria include acute skin or mucosal involvement with
respiratory, circulatory or severe gastrointestinal features as one
diagnostic route.

90
00:17:11,712 --> 00:17:23,232
Another route is acute hypotension, bronchospasm or laryngeal
involvement after a known or highly probable allergen for that
patient, even when the skin looks normal.

91
00:17:23,232 --> 00:17:35,568
The public RASH mnemonic helps remember affected systems, but one
isolated mild skin symptom is not automatically proof of anaphylaxis.
Assess the consequence and progression.

92
00:17:35,568 --> 00:17:46,128
Criteria support early clinical recognition; they are not permission
to delay care until every historical detail, rash or pressure
threshold is complete.

93
00:17:46,128 --> 00:17:54,432
Do not require a known previous allergy. The trigger or earlier
sensitization may be unknown even in a serious reaction.

94
00:17:54,432 --> 00:18:04,104
Do not require visible hives. Ask about earlier skin changes, but give
the current airway, breathing and perfusion pattern its proper weight.

95
00:18:04,104 --> 00:18:13,272
Do not require hypotension below ninety. Progressive airway or
respiratory compromise can make treatment urgent before that number is
reached.

96
00:18:13,272 --> 00:18:22,656
Keep a useful alternative in mind without delaying the indicated
action. Improvement or lack of improvement after treatment must
trigger another assessment.

97
00:18:22,656 --> 00:18:34,800
For recognized anaphylaxis, IM epinephrine is the first-line
medication. Give it promptly through the verified local route and
authorization while ABC support continues.

98
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:43,944
Assign work in parallel so drawing up an IV line or attaching the
monitor does not postpone the first indicated IM dose.

99
00:18:43,944 --> 00:18:55,008
Antihistamines and bronchodilators do not provide the same rescue.
Choosing a less alarming-looking drug first can leave the vascular or
upper-airway threat untreated.

100
00:18:55,008 --> 00:19:04,584
A cardiovascular history deserves monitoring and care, not automatic
withholding of indicated IM epinephrine in a life-threatening
situation.

101
00:19:04,584 --> 00:19:14,352
Alpha-adrenergic effects support vascular tone and reduce mucosal
swelling. These effects matter when return and airway caliber are
threatened.

102
00:19:14,352 --> 00:19:23,448
Beta-one effects support the heart, while beta-two effects relax
bronchial smooth muscle. This is more than treatment of an itchy rash.

103
00:19:23,448 --> 00:19:33,288
Epinephrine also limits mediator effects and release. An H-one
antihistamine cannot reproduce this combined airway and circulatory
action.

104
00:19:33,288 --> 00:19:44,160
Use the mechanism to predict useful endpoints: better voice/airflow,
effective breathing and improved perfusion, while monitoring adverse
effects and recurrence.

105
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:58,776
The checked local adult anaphylaxis schedule is zero point five
milligrams IM every five minutes, with its stated repeat instructions.
Reassess and follow the actual current protocol rather than invent a
dose ceiling.

106
00:19:58,776 --> 00:20:09,768
Pediatric anaphylaxis is weight based at zero point zero one
milligrams per kilogram, maximum zero point five milligrams, through
the actual pediatric pathway.

107
00:20:09,768 --> 00:20:21,864
The preferred site is the anterolateral middle thigh. The textbook
five-to-fifteen-minute interval and pediatric zero point three maximum
are not silently merged into the current local schedule.

108
00:20:21,864 --> 00:20:35,040
IM anaphylaxis, severe-bronchospasm treatment and advanced cardiac-
arrest IV or IO epinephrine are different indications and routes. A
shared drug name does not make them interchangeable.

109
00:20:35,040 --> 00:20:45,288
For this preparation exercise, the correct adult IM pathway calls for
zero point five milligrams and the actual label says one milligram per
milliliter.

110
00:20:45,288 --> 00:20:57,360
Divide the mass by the concentration: zero point five milligrams
divided by one milligram per milliliter equals zero point five
milliliters. State both mass and volume.

111
00:20:57,360 --> 00:21:09,720
Use the label in milligrams per milliliter, not only old ratio
notation. A zero point one milligram-per-milliliter arrest syringe is
ten times more dilute and is not the same product.

112
00:21:09,720 --> 00:21:22,440
Complete the independent check required by the actual process. Do not
import the inconsistent advanced-IV dilution paragraph from the book
or give the IM preparation as an IV bolus.

113
00:21:22,440 --> 00:21:31,296
Ask whether the patient or a helper used an autoinjector, and
establish the actual product, dose if known, time and response.

114
00:21:31,296 --> 00:21:40,680
That dose is part of the same treatment course. Do not reset the
medication clock at crew arrival or omit it from the record and
receiving report.

115
00:21:40,680 --> 00:21:52,200
Follow the actual device instructions rather than assuming every model
has the same fixed dose or hold time. Identify what supply remains
without postponing indicated care.

116
00:21:52,200 --> 00:22:05,688
A previous reaction, exposure without symptoms or a personalized
rescue plan needs context. Do not turn the textbook example into a
universal instruction to inject every asymptomatic exposure.

117
00:22:05,688 --> 00:22:17,424
Avoid standing or walking during anaphylaxis. Sudden upright movement
can further reduce venous return when vascular tone and circulating
volume are already compromised.

118
00:22:17,424 --> 00:22:27,768
Use a circulation-supporting supine position when tolerated, with a
suitable supported modification if severe breathing difficulty or
vomiting makes that unsafe.

119
00:22:27,768 --> 00:22:38,208
Pregnancy may require a suitable left-sided position or compression-
relief plan. Do not force one posture at the expense of effective
breathing or airway protection.

120
00:22:38,208 --> 00:22:49,752
Plan a coordinated stretcher movement, keep treatment available and
reassess immediately afterward. The fact that the patient feels better
is not permission to walk to the ambulance.

121
00:22:49,752 --> 00:23:01,008
Give oxygen and support ventilation according to the actual clinical
problem. A mask with oxygen flow does not prove adequate tidal
movement or carbon-dioxide clearance.

122
00:23:01,008 --> 00:23:13,128
If assisted ventilation is needed, use appropriate airway position,
seal, adjuncts and help. Monitor actual chest movement and response
rather than squeeze rate alone.

123
00:23:13,128 --> 00:23:24,840
Obstructed lungs need adequate exhalation. Forceful rapid bagging can
worsen air trapping, gastric distension, vomiting, pressure injury and
hypotension.

124
00:23:24,840 --> 00:23:39,192
Seek skilled airway resources early when swelling or fatigue makes
support ineffective. Advanced or surgical airway discussion does not
create independent PCP authority or replace IM epinephrine.

125
00:23:39,192 --> 00:23:47,904
Salbutamol can help persistent bronchospasm after epinephrine, but it
does not provide the same vascular or upper-airway effects.

126
00:23:47,904 --> 00:24:02,160
Diphenhydramine may improve urticaria, not life-threatening airway or
circulatory failure. Current E zero nine and the monograph place it at
ACP, unlike the saved examination page PCP heading.

127
00:24:02,160 --> 00:24:14,232
Steroids act slowly and routine prevention of biphasic reactions is
unproven. Neither an antihistamine nor a steroid justifies delaying
epinephrine or declaring the patient safe.

128
00:24:14,232 --> 00:24:25,776
Treat residual symptoms through the actual authorized plan and keep
reassessing the main threats. Persistent skin itch alone is not
equivalent to persistent circulatory collapse.

129
00:24:25,776 --> 00:24:37,392
An inadequate response requires another complete assessment. Confirm
what medicine actually reached the patient, the dose, route and time,
including earlier autoinjector treatment.

130
00:24:37,392 --> 00:24:51,048
Repeat the ABC and perfusion examination and consider ongoing exposure
or a competing diagnosis. Do not assume that silence, sedation or a
slightly better saturation means recovery.

131
00:24:51,048 --> 00:25:02,712
Follow the permitted repeat IM pathway with indicated fluids and
respiratory support. Call for advanced help early rather than
improvising an IV epinephrine bolus.

132
00:25:02,712 --> 00:25:18,288
The textbook advanced glucagon and IV dilution passages contain unsafe
unit or concentration problems. They are not rescue recipes, and beta-
blocker history does not create a new ordinary PCP glucagon
indication.

133
00:25:18,288 --> 00:25:27,672
Sepsis and anaphylaxis can both produce distributive circulatory
failure, but their history and immediate cause-specific decisions
differ.

134
00:25:27,672 --> 00:25:38,616
Do not give an IM anaphylaxis regimen to every septic patient because
the pressure is low, or postpone epinephrine in anaphylaxis while
searching for an infection source.

135
00:25:38,616 --> 00:25:50,544
Bleeding, cardiac or obstructive shock, glucose problems and toxins
may overlap the presentation. Select a useful alternative and a
discriminator that can alter care.

136
00:25:50,544 --> 00:26:02,976
The shared discipline is immediate support, accurate data, action-
response reassessment and early appropriate destination planning, not
forcing one diagnosis to fit every finding.

137
00:26:02,976 --> 00:26:58,368
Read the naloxone rows by route. The recovered March copy has a
separate three-milligram intranasal entry; the June 2026 copy says
four milligrams intranasally. Three milligrams is not a cumulative
maximum or an intravenous dose. Its separate IM/IV sequence is 0.4,
0.4, 0.8 and 2.0 milligrams at the stated reassessment intervals.
Original arithmetic: all four amounts sum to 3.6 milligrams, not four
equal 0.4-milligram doses. The current adult PCP monograph uses those
four escalating amounts every three minutes as needed and requires
CliniCall before further doses. Route, actual product and response
still matter; keep breathing support in parallel and never infer
pediatric permission from this adult schedule.

138
00:26:58,368 --> 00:27:57,408
For nitrate, the March copy ties a dose to the patient prescription
strength, requires oversight without a prescription, uses a strict
pressure greater than 110 for repeats and describes three doses in
thirty minutes. The later exam copy states 0.4 milligrams
sublingually; current BCEHS adult ACS instructions use 0.4 every three
to five minutes, pressure at least 110 and heart rate 50 to 150, with
CliniCall before use without a prior prescription or beyond three
doses. Original comparison: a reliable systolic value of exactly 110
does not meet an old greater-than-110 rule, even though it meets the
current numerical minimum. It still does not establish the indication,
other exclusions or authority. The source restart-after-pain-relief
note is not permission to reset a generic dose clock.

139
00:27:57,408 --> 00:28:05,808
At an outdoor pottery fair, a twenty-one-year-old develops throat
tightness, a hoarse voice and wheeze shortly after an insect sting.

140
00:28:05,808 --> 00:28:17,880
Pulse is one hundred twenty-two, pressure one hundred eight over
seventy and breathing work is increasing. No obvious hives are seen
and there is no previous diagnosed allergy.

141
00:28:17,880 --> 00:28:28,320
The acute exposure-associated airway and breathing pattern is
concerning even without rash or systolic pressure below ninety. Do not
wait for those missing findings.

142
00:28:28,320 --> 00:28:39,528
Recognize the indicated anaphylaxis pathway while assessing the
immediate airway and circulation. A statement of no known allergies
does not close that pathway.

143
00:28:39,528 --> 00:28:52,680
Organize prompt verified IM epinephrine and ABC support in parallel,
request appropriate help and prepare safe egress. Do not make IV
access a condition for the first IM dose.

144
00:28:52,680 --> 00:29:02,976
Use a suitable supported position and prevent standing or walking. If
removing the ongoing trigger is feasible, do it promptly without
delaying rescue.

145
00:29:02,976 --> 00:29:16,728
After treatment, compare voice, airway swelling, air entry, work,
mentation and perfusion with timed observations. A smaller rash is not
the main endpoint in this case.

146
00:29:16,728 --> 00:29:29,952
Repeat or escalate through the actual pathway if compromise persists.
Convey and prenotify despite improvement, including every dose and
response rather than only saying allergic reaction treated.

147
00:29:29,952 --> 00:29:39,936
Ask what actually changed after the intervention, with real
observations and timing. A treatment completed is not automatically a
treatment that worked.

148
00:29:39,936 --> 00:29:49,776
Predict the desired change from the mechanism: better airflow or
perfusion after indicated epinephrine, for example, not merely a
different pulse number.

149
00:29:49,776 --> 00:30:01,296
Look deliberately for worsening breathing, pressure, consciousness,
vomiting, recurrence or a delivery error. A quieter patient can be
improving or failing.

150
00:30:01,296 --> 00:30:12,240
Then choose whether to continue, modify, stop or escalate. State the
evidence for that next decision so the crew and receiving team can
follow the reasoning.

151
00:30:12,240 --> 00:30:33,912
The assigned article links emergency readiness to reliable medication
supplies. Verify the actual product, expiry, storage, delivery device
and trained role before use. An older US syringe-kit program is not
permission to improvise a replacement, change concentrations or reduce
a dose by half without authority.

152
00:30:33,912 --> 00:30:54,408
If a patient does not improve, reassess airway, breathing, perfusion,
actual delivery and the continuing reaction. Do not infer a single
cause from expiry alone or delay the current repeat and escalation
pathway while debating the device. Record what was actually given and
how the patient responded.

153
00:30:54,408 --> 00:31:07,032
Lead with suspected cause and the actual threat: infection with new
dysfunction, or exposure-associated airway and circulatory compromise,
rather than a long background narrative.

154
00:31:07,032 --> 00:31:19,536
Give the relevant timeline and current measured findings. For maternal
illness include the actual delivery or loss date; for anaphylaxis
include self-treatment and every epinephrine dose.

155
00:31:19,536 --> 00:31:30,984
State what was actually delivered and the response, including
persistent concerns. Do not turn a working syndrome into a confirmed
culture result or confirmed allergen diagnosis.

156
00:31:30,984 --> 00:31:42,672
Include ETA, needed capability and a clear request, then verify
understanding using the current transfer or consultation structure.
Match the same facts in the PCR.

157
00:31:42,672 --> 00:31:53,904
Use an observer to reveal only the information actually obtained. The
crew must request missing history or perform the relevant assessment
rather than assume normal findings.

158
00:31:53,904 --> 00:32:06,864
Introduce one changing variable, such as new hypotension, persistent
aphasia after glucose correction or wheeze after an exposure history.
Say what it changes and what remains uncertain.

159
00:32:06,864 --> 00:32:17,064
Practise clear medication mass, concentration, volume and route checks
with trainer supplies, plus non-walking movement and repeated
observations.

160
00:32:17,064 --> 00:32:28,608
Debrief a specific decision or communication gap and repeat the
changed process. Actual injection, airway or movement competence still
requires the school practical observer.

161
00:32:28,608 --> 00:32:38,904
You should now explain why sepsis may be concerning before fever or a
critical pressure appears, and use the school SIRS exercise without
calling it the modern definition.

162
00:32:38,904 --> 00:32:51,624
You should recognize anaphylaxis with the appropriate clinical pattern
even when skin findings or previous allergy history are absent, and
avoid labelling isolated mild hives as the same emergency.

163
00:32:51,624 --> 00:33:04,968
Deliver the indicated treatment through the actual safe route and
authority, support ABCs, move safely and evaluate the response. Setup
tasks and adjuncts must not steal the priority.

164
00:33:04,968 --> 00:33:17,808
Continue with the school toxicology, renal, gastrointestinal and
endocrine requirements. The paired reading supports this workshop but
does not certify every missing school slide or practical activity.
